THE PORT'S LARGEST ALLOCATION-COUNT SITE, HOISTED — AND REVERTED. 18-20 % OF
EVERY ALLOCATION GONE, 0.7 % OF aarch64 CPU BACK, PEAK RSS UNMOVED ON macOS AND
**+3.3 % WORSE ON LINUX**.

**THE CHANGE IS NOT IN THE TREE.** It was written, measured on four axes and two
ISAs, and reverted; this file is the measurement. Read §"THE VERDICT" first.

date:   2026-09-03
before: 673fbb22 (main)
after:  before + this commit (nothing else)
hosts:  CPU and RSS on mac (Apple silicon, macOS); allocation counts and peak
        heap on r7900x (x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, heaptrack).
cell:   gradient, qp 40, 8-bit 4:2:0, inter arm (SVTAV1_VIDEO=1
        SVTAV1_FRAMES=2 SVTAV1_FRAME_SHIFT=3 SVTAV1_INTER_EXPERIMENTAL=1).
data:   benchmarks/neighbor_scratch_ab_2026-09-03.tsv

=================== WHAT CHANGED =============================================

`partition::extract_neighbors_tiled` returns two `Vec<u8>` — the above row and
the left column of a block's intra reference samples, at most 64 bytes each —
and they die before the next block is predicted. It is called once per
predicted transform unit, which makes it **the port's single largest
ALLOCATION-COUNT site: 637,972 calls on one gradient 2048x2048 p13 two-frame
encode, 23.5 % of the whole process's allocations, carrying 128 B of peak
heap.**

`extract_neighbors_tiled_into` writes the same values into caller-owned
buffers; the allocating form is kept as a thin wrapper so the five cold call
sites and the tests are untouched. The hot caller,
`leaf_funnel::predict::predict_unit`, takes a thread-local `NeighborScratch`
(the shape `leaf_funnel::detect::txb_coeff_satd` already uses, including its
re-entrancy contract) which also absorbs the filter-intra arm's `above_c`.

Byte-inert by construction: every buffer is cleared and refilled to exactly the
length and contents the allocating form produced.

=================== MEASURED =================================================

ALLOCATIONS (heaptrack, whole process, 2048x2048 p13):
  videokey   2,496,391 -> 2,007,978    -488,413   0.804x
  inter      2,710,505 -> 2,210,640    -499,865   0.816x

PEAK HEAP (heaptrack, same cells): 83.10 M and 112.73 M, **unchanged to the
digit** — as expected, since the buffers were never live at the peak.

CPU (perf_encode ENCODE_NS over the whole 2-frame sequence, interleaved, median
of 9 paired rounds, after/before) — **AND IT IS ISA-SPLIT**:

  cell        aarch64-darwin    x86_64-linux
  512 p8          0.9944           1.0018
  1024 p8         0.9911           1.0062
  512 p13         0.9903           0.9925
  2048 p13        0.9941           0.9978

**aarch64: four cells, all below 1.0, 0.990-0.994 — a ~0.7 % win. x86: a
NULL** — two cells above 1.0 and two below, median ~1.00, which is what noise
looks like. Do not quote the aarch64 figure as the change's cost anywhere else.
The plausible reason for the split is that libmalloc's per-call cost is higher
than glibc's, so the same removed calls are worth more on the Mac; not
measured.

PEAK RSS (interleaved, paired rounds, median):
  macOS, 11 rounds        1536 inter   88512 ->  88080   0.995
                          2048 inter  150096 -> 152880   1.019
**NULL on macOS.** Both are inside the run-to-run spread, which on this host is
15 % ([149888, 171312] before and [147872, 168128] after at 2048).

  x86_64-linux, 7/15 rds  1536 inter   67596 ->  67500   0.999
                          2048 inter  114724 -> 118472   **1.033**
**A REGRESSION on Linux, and it is not noise.** Over fifteen paired rounds at
2048 the two distributions do not overlap at all — before spans
[114392, 115004] and after spans [118060, 118952]. Removing a fifth of the
process's allocations makes its peak resident set **3.7 MiB LARGER**.

Peak HEAP is unchanged to the digit on the same cells, so this is a
resident-page-layout effect, not live bytes: with 500k fewer small requests the
allocator satisfies the remaining large ones out of different chunks and the
heap's high-water rises. Not chased further — the direction is what matters.

=================== THE VERDICT: REVERTED =====================================

Four axes, two ISAs:

  allocations   -18 to -20 %          (measured)
  peak heap      0.000               (measured, both cells)
  CPU            -0.7 % on aarch64, NULL on x86   (measured, four cells each)
  peak RSS       NULL on macOS, **+3.3 % on Linux**  (measured)

**A memory chunk does not ship a 3.7 MiB peak-RSS regression for a 0.7 %
single-ISA CPU gain.** The change is reverted. What is kept is this file and the
null in §"WHY THE NULL MATTERS", which is the reason the work was worth
doing.

=================== WHY THE NULL MATTERS MORE THAN THE WIN ===================

`benchmarks/mem_churn_rss_2026-09-03.meta` measured ~100 bytes of
resident-not-live memory per allocation on macOS, correlating across two
presets. This change is the direct test of the causal reading of that number,
and on the biggest lever available — a fifth of every allocation the process
makes — it buys nothing. **That figure is a correlation, not a budget.**

The most likely reason it does not transfer: these are 64-byte allocations,
served out of libmalloc's tiny/nano magazines, which hand back
already-resident pages and never grow the resident set. Whatever scales the
macOS resident-not-live excess with mode-decision work, it is not the COUNT of
small allocations. A candidate consistent with both results is the SIZE
distribution — transient allocations large enough to take fresh regions — and
it is NOT measured.

Filed as a CPU result. Do not quote it on the memory axis.

=================== BYTE-CLEANLINESS, AS FAR AS IT WAS TAKEN =================

The change was byte-inert on everything that ran before it was withdrawn:

  aarch64-darwin  cargo nextest 2509/2509; regression_spotcheck 98/98;
                  inter_byte_gate 93 required / 0 failed / 4 known-open;
                  fctx_gate 96/96; inter_decode_gate 5/5;
                  inter_decode_census 96/96; video_key_matrix 58/60.
  x86_64-linux    cargo build --all-targets clean; cargo nextest 2519/2519.

`inter_completion_scan` and `identity_full_8bit` were STOPPED mid-run when the
Linux peak-RSS measurement came in, because the change was being reverted. They
are NOT reported as passes here. Nothing in this file depends on them: the
memory verdict is the reason for the revert and it does not need a byte gate.

=================== NOT ESTABLISHED ==========================================
  * WHY the Linux peak RSS rises. Measured cleanly, not attributed. The heap is
    unchanged, so it is placement, not live bytes.
  * A CPU WIN on x86. The four x86 cells read 1.0018 / 1.0062 / 0.9925 /
    0.9978 — a null. Only the aarch64 half is a win.
  * Any content but gradient, qp but 40, preset outside {8, 13}.
  * That the RSS null holds at other sizes or presets. Two cells, one preset.
